Third-party build and runtime dependencies with a non-Apache-2.0 licence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These do not taint the codebase above and are recorded here rather than left
for a scanner to find first:

  @img/sharp-* (an optional dependency of Next.js's image handling) bundles
  libvips, Apache-2.0 AND LGPL-3.0-or-later. The LGPL-3.0 half's obligations
  (notice, and the ability to relink) attach on conveyance of the built
  image. As of 2026-09-06 no workflow pushes the frontend image to any
  registry, so nothing is conveyed today; see "Running your own instance" in
  README.md if that ever changes.

  lightningcss (a Tailwind CSS v4 build-time dependency) and axe-core /
  @axe-core/playwright (e2e/ test-only) are MPL-2.0, file-level reciprocal,
  and neither is modified or shipped.

  WebPush 1.0.13 (.NET, server-side push notifications) is MPL-2.0 per its
  upstream repository (github.com/web-push-libs/web-push-csharp/blob/master/LICENSE);
  its own NuGet package metadata records only a deprecated licenseUrl. Used
  unmodified.

  @sentry/cli, pulled in transitively by @sentry/nextjs and used only to
  upload source maps at build time, is FSL-1.1-MIT (the Functional Source
  License): source-available but not OSI-approved, and the only licence
  here that restricts what the software may be used for. It is a build-time
  tool: Next's standalone output (what Dockerfile.frontend actually copies
  into the runtime image) keeps @sentry/nextjs and drops @sentry/cli, so it
  does not reach the shipped image the way the LGPL-3.0 libvips payload
  above does. `npm ci` against this repository does pull it, so a fork that
  vendors node_modules redistributes it.

